  "does_not_prove": [
    "Concurrency 1 is service time. At higher concurrency throughput barely rises above concurrency 1, so something single-threaded is saturated and most of each latency is queueing. Whether that is the one API worker or the one-process load generator was not separated; both are single Python event loops on one machine.",
    "The load generator ran on the same machine as the API, PostgreSQL and Redis and competed with them for CPU; a remote client would see network time added and less contention.",
    "One API process with one worker. It says nothing about horizontal scaling.",
    "Only the four market endpoints are cached; the authenticated endpoints are listed in both runs as a control and should not differ beyond noise.",
    "A small, fixed dataset. Bars for one symbol at the default window; latency on a much larger table or a cold OS page cache will differ."
  ]
}
